    {
      "id": 131647,
      "name": "Silverleaf",
      "username": "Silverleaf",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
      "created_at": "2015-02-17T05:56:04.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Matchstickman\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/matchstickman/48/1880_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Matchstickman:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<div><b>Silverleaf wrote:</b><br><blockquote>What do you mean by \"the material cost\"?The game would cost the same to develop whether you had bought a previous version or not. And no store in the world would discount a CD album for you because you bought the cassette version back in the 80s.</blockquote></div>\nTrue, but quiet a few RPG companies discount the cost of the PDF from the physical version of their product, or out right give away the PDF if you buy the physical product. \n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>But it's an easy (and quick) job to convert your print file to pdf, so it essentially cost the company very little. They can afford to give it away. It's a bit like Adam's art prints - already \"paid\" for since the work's already done, so they only really cost paper and ink.</p>\n<p>I dread to think how many people hours go into converting a game into console format (or any other format), and hours cost money. It's certainly not a thing you can do with a click of a \"save as pdf/print to pdf\" button, and it's not something that's already done like Adam's art.</p>\n<p>Also, how do you prove you own a physical copy of a game that's sold through stores via distributers? What about second-hand or traded copies?</p>",
      "post_number": 7,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:06:59.710Z",
