      "raw": "[quote=\"Matchstickman\"]\n\n<div class=\"bb-quote\"><b>Silverleaf wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class=\"bb-quote-body\">What do you mean by &quot;the material cost&quot;?The game would cost the same to develop whether you had bought a previous version or not. And no store in the world would discount a CD album for you because you bought the cassette version back in the 80s.</blockquote></div>\nTrue, but quiet a few RPG companies discount the cost of the PDF from the physical version of their product, or out right give away the PDF if you buy the physical product. \n[/quote]\n \n\nBut it&#39;s an easy (and quick) job to convert your print file to pdf, so it essentially cost the company very little. They can afford to give it away. It&#39;s a bit like Adam&#39;s art prints - already &quot;paid&quot; for since the work&#39;s already done, so they only really cost paper and ink.\n\nI dread to think how many people hours go into converting a game into console format (or any other format), and hours cost money. It&#39;s certainly not a thing you can do with a click of a &quot;save as pdf/print to pdf&quot; button, and it&#39;s not something that&#39;s already done like Adam&#39;s art.\n\nAlso, how do you prove you own a physical copy of a game that&#39;s sold through stores via distributers? What about second-hand or traded copies?\n",
